1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,077
A closed bottle of water fogs up inside.

2
00:00:03,077 --> 00:00:06,358
A hot kiln turns chalky
stone into lime and a gas.

3
00:00:06,758 --> 00:00:09,560
Solids, liquids and gases,
all in one equilibrium.

4
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:11,210
So which of them go into K?

5
00:00:11,660 --> 00:00:14,466
Diya covers a tumbler
of water in her kitchen.

6
00:00:14,466 --> 00:00:16,472
Tiny drops gather under the lid.

7
00:00:16,822 --> 00:00:21,554
A full tumbler or a nearly empty one,
the air above holds the same amount

8
00:00:21,554 --> 00:00:22,454
of vapour.

9
00:00:22,930 --> 00:00:26,966
By the end, you will know why some
substances simply drop out of K.

10
00:00:27,616 --> 00:00:29,388
First, one new word.

11
00:00:29,388 --> 00:00:30,288
Phase.

12
00:00:30,418 --> 00:00:33,135
A phase is one even part of a mixture.

13
00:00:33,135 --> 00:00:35,731
One solid, one liquid, or one gas.

14
00:00:36,131 --> 00:00:38,599
Ice floating in water has two phases.

15
00:00:38,599 --> 00:00:41,732
Air is one phase, even
with many gases in it.

16
00:00:42,132 --> 00:00:46,343
An equilibrium with more than one
phase is called heterogeneous.

17
00:00:46,343 --> 00:00:47,986
Hetero means different.

18
00:00:48,586 --> 00:00:52,175
Let's put a closed jar of
water on our molecule bench.

19
00:00:52,525 --> 00:00:55,940
Fast molecules escape from the
surface into the air above.

20
00:00:55,940 --> 00:00:57,242
That is evaporation.

21
00:00:57,642 --> 00:01:00,556
Some vapour molecules hit
the water and stick back.

22
00:01:00,556 --> 00:01:01,848
That is condensation.

23
00:01:02,248 --> 00:01:03,967
Soon both rates are equal.

24
00:01:03,967 --> 00:01:06,104
The vapour pressure stops changing.

25
00:01:06,504 --> 00:01:08,397
Now take out half the water.

26
00:01:08,397 --> 00:01:11,053
The vapour pressure
stays exactly the same.

27
00:01:11,453 --> 00:01:14,937
So K here is just the
pressure of the water vapour.

28
00:01:14,937 --> 00:01:16,659
K p equals p of water.

29
00:01:17,259 --> 00:01:19,220
Why does the liquid drop out?

30
00:01:19,220 --> 00:01:20,934
Think of a chocolate bar.

31
00:01:21,284 --> 00:01:22,395
Cut it in half.

32
00:01:22,395 --> 00:01:25,433
Each piece is smaller, but
packed just as tightly.

33
00:01:25,833 --> 00:01:30,759
For a pure solid or a pure liquid, the
packing is its concentration, moles

34
00:01:30,759 --> 00:01:31,659
per litre.

35
00:01:31,654 --> 00:01:34,791
It never changes, so we leave it out of K.

36
00:01:35,441 --> 00:01:38,485
Let's see it with the
lime kiln, step by step.

37
00:01:38,835 --> 00:01:39,800
Step one.

38
00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:44,989
Solid calcium carbonate breaks into
solid calcium oxide and carbon dioxide

39
00:01:44,989 --> 00:01:45,889
gas.

40
00:01:46,339 --> 00:01:47,239
Step two.

41
00:01:47,237 --> 00:01:49,098
Write K the normal way.

42
00:01:49,098 --> 00:01:53,236
Calcium oxide times carbon
dioxide, over calcium carbonate.

43
00:01:53,986 --> 00:01:54,886
Step three.

44
00:01:54,854 --> 00:01:57,166
Both solids have fixed concentrations.

45
00:01:57,166 --> 00:01:59,205
We fold them into the constant.

46
00:01:59,955 --> 00:02:00,936
Step four.

47
00:02:00,936 --> 00:02:04,172
K c equals the carbon
dioxide concentration.

48
00:02:04,172 --> 00:02:07,656
And K p equals the
pressure of carbon dioxide.

49
00:02:08,406 --> 00:02:09,493
Now three more examples.

50
00:02:09,843 --> 00:02:14,736
Solid nickel and carbon monoxide gas
make a nickel gas, used to purify

51
00:02:14,736 --> 00:02:15,636
nickel.

52
00:02:15,465 --> 00:02:17,563
The solid nickel drops out.

53
00:02:17,963 --> 00:02:20,054
Silver oxide with nitric acid.

54
00:02:20,054 --> 00:02:22,839
The solid and the liquid water drop out.

55
00:02:22,839 --> 00:02:25,185
Only the dissolved substances stay.

56
00:02:25,585 --> 00:02:29,411
Carbon dioxide with solid
carbon gives carbon monoxide.

57
00:02:29,411 --> 00:02:31,335
Only the two gases stay.

58
00:02:31,735 --> 00:02:32,635
The rule.

59
00:02:32,379 --> 00:02:34,225
Keep gases and dissolved substances.

60
00:02:34,225 --> 00:02:36,039
Drop pure solids and pure liquids.

61
00:02:36,639 --> 00:02:37,539
Compare.

62
00:02:37,302 --> 00:02:41,793
A big lump of chalky stone and a small
lump, in two closed boxes, at the

63
00:02:41,793 --> 00:02:42,932
same temperature.

64
00:02:43,332 --> 00:02:44,502
Pause and think.

65
00:02:44,502 --> 00:02:47,526
Which box ends with more
carbon dioxide pressure?

66
00:02:50,726 --> 00:02:51,626
The same.

67
00:02:51,452 --> 00:02:55,746
The solid is not in K, so the gas
pressure is fixed at that temperature.

68
00:02:56,396 --> 00:02:57,929
One important catch.

69
00:02:58,279 --> 00:03:01,871
Some solid must stay in
the box, even a tiny bit.

70
00:03:01,871 --> 00:03:05,246
If it is all used up,
there is no equilibrium.

71
00:03:05,646 --> 00:03:07,270
NCERT gives a real value.

72
00:03:07,270 --> 00:03:11,212
At eleven hundred kelvin, the carbon
dioxide pressure is two times ten

73
00:03:11,212 --> 00:03:12,557
to the five pascals.

74
00:03:12,957 --> 00:03:14,937
A pascal is a unit of pressure.

75
00:03:14,937 --> 00:03:18,157
Divide by one bar, which
is ten to the five pascals.

76
00:03:18,557 --> 00:03:22,944
So K p equals two point zero
zero, at that temperature only.

77
00:03:23,544 --> 00:03:24,444
Remember this.

78
00:03:24,213 --> 00:03:25,856
Pure solids and liquids stay out of K.

79
00:03:26,556 --> 00:03:28,246
Now, one worked example.

80
00:03:28,546 --> 00:03:32,152
Carbon dioxide plus carbon
gives two carbon monoxide.

81
00:03:32,152 --> 00:03:35,459
K p is three point zero
at a thousand kelvin.

82
00:03:35,459 --> 00:03:40,195
We start with zero point four eight
bar of carbon dioxide and plenty of

83
00:03:40,195 --> 00:03:41,095
carbon.

84
00:03:41,399 --> 00:03:43,877
Let x bar of carbon dioxide react.

85
00:03:43,877 --> 00:03:49,155
It leaves zero point four eight minus
x, and makes two x of carbon monoxide.

86
00:03:49,905 --> 00:03:54,112
K p equals two x squared over
zero point four eight minus x.

87
00:03:54,112 --> 00:03:55,500
That equals three.

88
00:03:55,500 --> 00:03:56,837
Carbon stays out.

89
00:03:57,587 --> 00:03:58,487
Solve.

90
00:03:58,210 --> 00:04:02,636
Four x squared plus three x minus
one point four four equals zero.

91
00:04:02,636 --> 00:04:05,273
So x is about zero point three three.

92
00:04:06,023 --> 00:04:09,236
Carbon monoxide is zero point six six bar.

93
00:04:09,236 --> 00:04:12,450
Carbon dioxide is zero point one five bar.

94
00:04:13,100 --> 00:04:14,561
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:04:14,911 --> 00:04:18,890
Students put the solid into K, with
calcium carbonate on the bottom.

96
00:04:19,290 --> 00:04:20,390
Leave it out.

97
00:04:20,390 --> 00:04:23,385
K p is just the pressure
of carbon dioxide.

98
00:04:23,385 --> 00:04:27,102
Liquid water stays out too,
but water vapour stays in.

99
00:04:27,752 --> 00:04:29,103
Where is this asked?

100
00:04:29,103 --> 00:04:33,563
Boards ask you to write K c or K p
for a reaction with solids or liquids.

101
00:04:33,563 --> 00:04:36,772
Entrance tests ask for
pressures from a total pressure.

102
00:04:37,372 --> 00:04:38,636
Exam level now.

103
00:04:38,636 --> 00:04:42,812
Solid ammonium hydrogen sulphide
is heated in a closed flask.

104
00:04:42,812 --> 00:04:46,496
It breaks into ammonia gas
and hydrogen sulphide gas.

105
00:04:46,496 --> 00:04:50,511
At equilibrium the total
pressure is zero point eight bar.

106
00:04:50,511 --> 00:04:51,454
Find K p.

107
00:04:51,454 --> 00:04:52,771
Pause and solve.

108
00:04:58,171 --> 00:04:59,586
The answer is B.

109
00:04:59,586 --> 00:05:01,954
Zero point one six bar squared.

110
00:05:02,454 --> 00:05:04,015
The solid stays out.

111
00:05:04,015 --> 00:05:08,580
K p equals the ammonia pressure
times the hydrogen sulphide pressure.

112
00:05:09,080 --> 00:05:13,264
The two gases form one to one,
so each is half the total.

113
00:05:13,264 --> 00:05:14,831
Zero point four bar.

114
00:05:15,331 --> 00:05:19,312
Zero point four times zero point
four is zero point one six.

115
00:05:19,312 --> 00:05:21,318
A squared the total pressure.

116
00:05:21,918 --> 00:05:23,458
Here is your recap.

117
00:05:23,458 --> 00:05:25,041
Screenshot this card.

118
00:05:28,241 --> 00:05:30,427
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

119
00:05:30,827 --> 00:05:31,727
One.

120
00:05:31,287 --> 00:05:33,533
Why is a pure solid left out of K?

121
00:05:34,333 --> 00:05:35,233
Two.

122
00:05:34,807 --> 00:05:37,623
Write K p for calcium
carbonate breaking down.

123
00:05:38,423 --> 00:05:39,323
Three.

124
00:05:38,953 --> 00:05:42,116
Does adding more liquid water
change its vapour pressure?

125
00:05:43,016 --> 00:05:46,537
Next time: K is huge for one
reaction and tiny for another.

126
00:05:46,537 --> 00:05:48,026
What does that tell us?

127
00:05:48,026 --> 00:05:50,986
We find out in Applications
of Equilibrium Constants.
